<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>OpenVSA.Dsp</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyTitle>OpenVSA.Dsp</AssemblyTitle>
    <Description>L3 analysis core: FFT providers, window functions, resampling, filtering, averaging, statistics and gating.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    REQ-ARC-001: this assembly must NOT reference OpenVSA.Hal.Visa, .File or .Sim.
    It consumes IqBlock values only and cannot tell a live instrument from a file or simulator.
    OpenVSA.Architecture.Tests enforces this.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\OpenVSA.Core\OpenVSA.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    REQ-NFR-004 requires the active FFT provider to be selectable by configuration rather than by
    recompiling, so FftProviders reads appSettings.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Reference Include="System.Configuration" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    REQ-NFR-004's native provider. Built by src\OpenVSA.Dsp.Native and copied beside this assembly
    so the P/Invoke resolves with no deployment step.

    Not a ProjectReference: a .vcxproj referenced from a C# project makes MSBuild try to resolve an
    assembly from it, and there is none. The dependency is expressed as a build ordering plus a
    copied file, which is what a native dependency actually is.

    ContinueOnError, because REQ-NFR-004 makes the native provider optional: a machine with no C++
    toolchain still builds the product and runs on the managed default, registering one provider
    instead of two.
  -->
  <!--
    REQ-NFR-003's vector kernels. System.Numerics.Vectors carries Vector<float> on .NET Framework;
    without it the type resolves to the non-accelerated fallback in mscorlib and every measurement
    would compare scalar code against scalar code wearing a vector's name.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Numerics.Vectors" Version="4.6.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <OpenVsaNativeFft>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Dsp.Native\bin\x64\$(Configuration)\OpenVSA.Fft.Native.dll</OpenVsaNativeFft>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Full MSBuild only. The dotnet CLI's MSBuild has no C++ targets, so evaluating a .vcxproj under
    it fails with MSB4278 before any of this runs. That is also why OpenVSA.Dsp.Native is not in
    OpenVSA.slnx: "dotnet test OpenVSA.slnx" evaluates every project in the solution, C++ or not.
    The native library is built here, by the full-MSBuild build that precedes the tests.
  -->
  <Target Name="BuildNativeFft" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild"
          Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' != 'Core'">
    <MSBuild Projects="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\OpenVSA.Dsp.Native\OpenVSA.Dsp.Native.vcxproj"
             Properties="Configuration=$(Configuration);Platform=x64"
             ContinueOnError="true" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    A build that cannot produce the native library says so here, rather than leaving one test to
    report it as "Unable to load DLL OpenVSA.Fft.Native.dll" three minutes later.

    REQ-NFR-004 requires two providers, so a managed-only build is not a lesser configuration of the
    product — it is a build that does not meet the requirement. The trap this closes is that
    "dotnet test OpenVSA.slnx" is a natural thing to type, rebuilds everything under the Core
    MSBuild, drops the native library that a previous full-MSBuild build had put there, and fails a
    single unrelated-looking test. I did exactly that and spent the time working out why.

    Not fired when the library is already present: build once with MSBuild and "dotnet test" then
    works for the rest of the day, which is the flow worth keeping.
  -->
  <Target Name="RequireNativeFft" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild"
          Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' == 'Core' And !Exists('$(OpenVsaNativeFft)')">
    <Error Text="OpenVSA.Fft.Native.dll has not been built, and the dotnet CLI cannot build it: its MSBuild has no C++ targets, so OpenVSA.Dsp.Native.vcxproj fails to evaluate (MSB4278). REQ-NFR-004 requires a native FFT provider alongside the managed one, so this build would not meet the requirement. Build once with full MSBuild — msbuild OpenVSA.slnx /p:Configuration=$(Configuration) /p:Platform=x64 — after which 'dotnet test --no-build' and plain 'dotnet test' both work." />
  </Target>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      Under full MSBuild the item is unconditional, NOT conditioned on Exists(). Item conditions are
      evaluated before any target runs, so on a clean checkout Exists() is false at evaluation time,
      because BuildNativeFft has not produced the library yet. The item is dropped and the copy
      silently never happens. That is what made CI report "Unable to load DLL
      OpenVSA.Fft.Native.dll" from a build that had compiled it moments earlier.

      Under the dotnet CLI nothing can build it, so Exists() is then exactly the right question:
      either an earlier MSBuild build left it there and it is copied, or RequireNativeFft above has
      already stopped the build.
    -->
    <Content Include="$(OpenVsaNativeFft)"
             Condition="'$(MSBuildRuntimeType)' != 'Core' Or Exists('$(OpenVsaNativeFft)')">
      <Link>OpenVSA.Fft.Native.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
